From: rstevens@noao.edu (W. Richard Stevens)
Subject: Re: sendmsg returns ENOBUFS (UDP & broadcast)
Date: 1996/03/25
Message-ID: <4j6s4e$84m@noao.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 144196762
references: <3156EF4D.177F623F@worldnet.fr>
organization: National Optical Astronomy Observatories, Tucson, AZ, USA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.ultrix,comp.protocols.tcp-ip

> On Digital UNIX 3.2, sendmsg on a UDP sock_dgram socket return
> ENOBUFS (broadcast mode), the manual page specifies that :
> The system was unable to allocate an internal buffer.
>
> I wonder if this is related to the sending buffer associated with
> the socket (which can be tuned by the use of setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF)
> or if this is due to a kernel buffer overflow. In the last case,
> can I increase this buffer, and what is the associated kernel variable ?

I've never seen Digital Unix, but on Berkeley-derived kernels, this
means the interface output queue was full.  UDP does not save a copy
of the outgoing datagrams so there really isn't a "socket send buffer"
for a UDP socket: the size you set with SO_SNDBUF just specifies the
largest UDP datagram that you can send on the socket.

Try "netstat -id" if Digital Unix supports it, to see how many packets
have been dropped by the interface on output.  If this is the problem,
