From: carlson@xylogics.com (James Carlson)
Subject: Re: sendmsg returns ENOBUFS (UDP & broadcast)
Date: 1996/03/25
Message-ID: <4j73bf$as6@newhub.xylogics.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 144207188
references: <3156EF4D.177F623F@worldnet.fr>
to: Laurent Deniel <deniel@worldnet.fr>
organization: Xylogics Incorporated
reply-to: carlson@xylogics.com
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.ultrix,comp.protocols.tcp-ip


In article <3156EF4D.177F623F@worldnet.fr>, Laurent Deniel <deniel@worldnet.fr> writes:
|> On Digital UNIX 3.2, sendmsg on a UDP sock_dgram socket return
|> ENOBUFS (broadcast mode), the manual page specifies that :
|> The system was unable to allocate an internal buffer.
|> 
|> I wonder if this is related to the sending buffer associated with
|> the socket (which can be tuned by the use of setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF)
|> or if this is due to a kernel buffer overflow. In the last case,
|> can I increase this buffer, and what is the associated kernel variable ?

No, that's not causing it.

There are two possible causes of ENOBUFS -- the kernel couldn't find or
allocate an mbuf (unlikely with most Unix systems) or the interface
output queue itself is full (quite likely with most UDP applications).

Unfortunately, most BSD-derived systems return ENOBUFS when the output
queue limit is reached.  That limit defaults to 50 packets and usually
is not configurable.  (In our kernel, we hacked this to return a new
and more meaningful error, ENOQUEUE, but this doesn't help you much ...)

You need to start pacing your output with a timer, or just accept the
inevitable packet loss as just part of the deal you make when using UDP.

