I am just about to set up a DEC 3000-700 as an NFS server. The machine
is eqipped with 64 MB of RAM, five RZ29B (two internal and the
other three mounted in a StorageWorks cabinet), and a TLZsomething DAT
autoloader. This server is intended to provide user home directories
for staff and students (about 7 GB each), quite a lot of shared
software (CAD, FEM and other) for DEC as well as HP boxes. The shared
application part i think will account for about 4 GB.

Anybody here got some good advice or thoughts on setting up Advfs (and
maybe LSM as well) on a system like this. I myself have identified at
least two possible configurations. Both of these include using one of
the disks as system disk which should not be included in any advfs
file domain or LSM volume. Spare space on this disk could be used for
shared software or something like that. For the other four disks I am
thinking about either  (1) linking together disks two by two into LSM
striped volumes which I then would tie together into one Advfs file
domain or (2) skip the LSM part entirely and build the advfs domain on
rrz[1,2,3,4]c. The domain itself would be divided into something like
big_domain#staff, big_domain#student and big_domain#sw.

I imagine that with the first configuration I would yield higher
performance at a cost of less flexibility on behalf of Avdfs - i.e. if
I would detect that a disk is about to shake off its mortal coil but
still works it would be easier to replace that disk if it is
standalone rather than if it is striped together with another disk.

Comments and opinions are greatly appreciated - especially when advfs
and LSM stability issues are concerned (I would hate to see my
advfs/LSM disk array blow up in my face not due to disk failure but to
bugs in the software).

Thanks!
/Erik

