In article <4j6q8e$ucf@xensei3.xensei.com> ccrlphr@xensei.com writes:
>I got a problem while using the dynamic loading with C++ (DEC CXX 5.0). 
>I wrote a app which defines an interface == class within a header file
>and lets you dynamically load libraries with this interface within the 
>run of the prog, e.g.:

Cool.


>...  My problem starts if I don't need the lib any longer and want to get the
>memory back using <dlclose>.  ...

The file /usr/lib/cmplrs/cxx/DECCXX500.release-notes states on pp. 1-10 and
1-11:

"
        1.3.3 Current Restrictions

              This section contains usage notes and known problems with
              DEC C++ Version 5.0.
...
              o  Static destructors in dynamically loaded DEC C++ shared
                 libraries are not invoked if the DEC OSF/1 Alpha library
                 routine dlclose(3) is used to dynamically unload the C++
                 shared libraries. Furthermore, the main program fails
                 when exiting because it attempts to call the destructors
                 in the unloaded shared libraries.
"

Sorry about that.


>This generates the question if this is
>        a) a general problem between C++ and the dl-library
>        b) a problem between DEC CXX (V5.0) and the dl-library
>        c) a problem on DEC UNIX (osf/1 V.3.2C)
>        d) a problem of misusage
>        e) nothing of the above

It's a day-one design flaw in the C++ runtime support code which executes
static object destructors upon program exit (and dlclose).

The problem is fixed in DEC C++/Alpha/Unix V5.3, scheduled to ship on CDrom on
12-Apr-96.


>BTW, does anybody knows a workaround ?? (except "not calling <dlopen>")

Will disabling the dlclose calls for a few more weeks suffice?
