In article <Pine.OSF.3.91.960321112642.1799A-100000@cosmic.ps.uci.edu>, Eric Schneider <schneid@cosmic.ps.uci.edu> writes:

>Help! I'm running OSF/1 3.2 on a DEC Alpha 3000/400. When running a process
>that is disk I/O intensive, our system response for other processes slows
>enormously. For example, it make take 20 seconds to change the active window
>on the console.  This occurs even with the I/O intensive program nice'd to
>19. All our disks are on the same SCSI bus, including the system disk. 
>
>System response is fine with several CPU bound processes running 
>concurrently.
>
>Is this considered typical?  Is there some way to correct this situation?

Digital UNIX uses a buffer cache implementation that consider all of
available memory free game when filling the cache.  The result is
that when reading and writing large files, idle pages will taken
from processes to satisfy the needs of the buffer cache.  As those
pages become active, they'll be taken back from the cache with the
attendent pageing I/O.  If the cache memory happens to be dirty
then it will have to flushed as well, increasing the overall I/O
load even more.

If the sort of I/O that causes this problem is common, then you can
adjust the maximum amount of memory that the buffer cache will try
to use.  The System Tuning and Performance Management guide descibes
a number of parameters that control the Unified Buffer Cache's use
of memory.  The sysconfigtab parameter "ubc-maxpercent" controls
the amount of memory that the cache will try to use.  Other parameters
can be used to control the amount of memory that any one file will
attempt to use.

>
