In article <DoJ64z.6A@bcstec.ca.boeing.com>, hen6940@bcstec.ca.boeing.com (Howard Nebeck) writes:
|>Hello,
|>    Sprinkled around our Dec_Unix system are files named ".new.."<somename>
|>and ".proto.."<somename> .  Obviously these are default copies (in some sense)
|>for the corresponding <somename> file.  However, I didn't find any mention of
|>them in our system admin manual, nor in the FAQ.  Surprisingly, one such file
|>"/var/spool/cron/crontabs/.new..root" seems to be spawning crontab jobs.
|>
|>    Can someone elaborate on what these files are supposed to be, and where
|>I can find further information about them?
|>
|>Thanks,
|>Howard Nebeck   (206) 662-0237   hen6940@cowboy.ds.boeing.com
|>

The quickest and easiest way to explain them is that they are needed for update
installations.  By delivering .new files, we don't directly overwrite
your configuration files when installing the next release during an update
installation.  You'll note that those files that we expect a customer to
modify will have an accompanying .new file.

The .proto files are used for dataless environments.

While you don't need either to run on a daily business, removing them will
likely cause any subsequent update installations to break.

Dave
