From: jonas@msiw38.msi.se (Jonas Hvarfner)
Subject: Re: [HELP] Formatting man pages using groff
Date: 1996/03/18
Message-ID: <4ijcuj$5j@msiw38.msi.se>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 143351574
references: <4i9lrt$r8a@mrnews.mro.dec.com>
content-type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
organization: Detp. of Atomic Physics, Univ. of Stockholm, Sweden
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


The following shellscript uses groff to print manual pages. Groff
needs some additional macro (-mad) information to produce good output.
The macros are included below. I keep the macro file (tmac.ad) in my
~/lib and use -M to tell groff where to look for it. The -t and -e
switches tells groff to run its input through eqn and tbl (or geqn or
gtbl). 

Additinonally, the pages get printed double sided, with changed page
offset. That is done by pstops by Angus Duggan. Use archie to find it.
If you don't want/need double sided printing, just send the output
from groff to lpr.

Hope this helps! Regards,
				Jonas Hvarfner
				jonas@atom.msi.se


#!/bin/sh
# Print manual pages nicely. Jonas Hvarfner 941110.
if [ $# -lt 1 ]; then
   echo Doublesided printing of manual pages. Output goes to ps5.
   echo Syntax: $0 path/manpage.mansect
   echo Example: $0 /usr/share/man/man1/f77.1
   exit
fi

groff -t -e -M/users/jonas/lib -mad -man $1 > /tmp/$$ 
pstops "2:0,1(-0.5cm,0cm)" /tmp/$$ | lpr -Plps17 -K2
echo Spooled to lps17.
rm /tmp/$$
# End of shellscript

Here follow the macros in tmac.ad. I took them from Digitals tmac.man.

.de EE		\" End example
.in -\\n(EXu
.fi
.}f             \" restore basic text attributes
.if n .sp 1
.if t .sp .5v
..
.de MS
.ie t \&\f5\\$1\fP\fR(\\$2)\fP\\$3
.el \fI\\$1\fP(\\$2)\\$3
..
.de PN
.ie t \&\f5\\$1\fP\\$2
.el \fI\\$1\fP\\$2
..


