                     FIRST CALL FOR VOTES (of 2)
                   unmoderated group comp.unix.cde

Newsgroups line:
comp.unix.cde		The Common Desktop Environment.

Votes must be received by 23:59:59 UTC, 10 Apr 1996.

This vote is being conducted by a neutral third party.  Questions about
this proposal should be directed to the proponent.

Proponent: Daniel Dardailler <daniel@x.org>
Proponent: Bob Scheifler <rws@x.org>
Proponent: Ken Flowers <flowers@osf.org>
Votetaker: Warren Lavallee <warren@Syra.NET>


RATIONALE: comp.unix.cde

The CDE topic is currently being discussed among several Unix
operating system specific groups, such as comp.unix.solaris,
comp.unix.aix, comp.sys.hp.hpux, comp.unix.osf.osf1, ergo readers
exist and would use a newsgroup about CDE if there was one.

It is important to note that most CDE threads in the above OS specific
group are often only relevant to the CDE technology itself, and not to
a particular Unix variant. The fact that CDE users have to follow
multiple platform specific groups to get information and answers about
CDE merely defeats the purpose of CDE itself: which is unification.

As noted in the header, there exists a CDE specific group in the Alt
hierarchy already , but its parenting under "windows" makes it such
that a lot of MS/Windows messages are sent to this group by mistake
(and CDE is an X- and Unix-based technology). Several times in the
past year, people have requested the creation of a "real" CDE group.

In the same alt.windows.cde forum were also discussed the rationales
for having a comp.unix.cde group, vs. a comp.windows.x.cde group. The
idea is that although CDE is mostly perceived as an extension of the X
and the Motif technology (visually it is), it encompasses more than
that in reality: ToolTalk, Calendar Management, and a Mail are
examples of services provided by CDE, and the CDE applications
directly extends the "standard" set of Unix utilitaries (CDE is an
X/Open standard).

CHARTER: comp.unix.cde

The charter for the CDE group is to discuss technical matters relevant
to the CDE, Common Desktop Environment.

CDE is a technology that unifies access, both programmatic and user,
to Unix desktop services such as windowing, graphics, and user
interface presentation, file and session management, and
inter-application messaging (a good introduction can be found at
http://www.austin.ibm.com/software/CDE/TOC/Intro/cdeintro.html).

An important aspect, since CDE is a "compound" technology, is that it
is not meant to replace the existing groups that already cover
individual parts of CDE: comp.windows.x.motif for instance, or
alt.soft-sys.tooltalk.

In other words, if the symptoms of the problem that a user is
reporting, or more generally if the matters presented is applicable to
one of the individual technology that forms CDE out of the CDE
context, it is better to only post to the specific group.

I can give more details about the CDE parts and their corresponding
existing Usenet group if requested.

END CHARTER.


HOW TO VOTE

Send E-MAIL to:   voting@syra.net

Your e-mail message should contain one and only one of the following
statements:

      I vote YES on comp.unix.cde
      I vote NO on comp.unix.cde

You may also vote ABSTAIN or CANCEL; these are not counted as votes and
don't affect the vote count.

Anything else may be rejected by the automatic vote counting program.
The votetaker will respond to your received ballots with a personal
acknowledge- ment by mail - if you do not receive one within several
days, try again. It's your responsibility to make sure your vote is
registered correctly.

If you later change your mind you may vote again; only your last valid
vote will count.

VOTING PROCEDURE NOTES:

Standard Guidelines for voting apply.  One person, one vote.  Votes must
be mailed directly from the voter to the votetaker.  Anonymous,
forwarded or proxy votes are not valid; this includes votes generated by
WWW/HTML/CGI forms.

Vote counting is automated.  Failure to follow these directions may mean
that your vote does not get counted.  If you do not receive an
acknowledgment of your vote within three days, contact the votetaker
about the problem.  It's your responsibility to make sure your vote is
registered correctly.  Duplicate votes are resolved in favor of the most
recent valid vote.  Addresses and votes of all voters will be published
in the final voting results post.

The purpose of a Usenet vote is to determine the genuine interest of
persons who would read a proposed newsgroup.  Soliciting votes from
disinterested parties defeats this purpose.  Please do not distribute
this CFV.  If you must, direct people to the official CFV as posted to
news.announce.newgroups.  Distributing pre-marked or otherwise edited
copies of this CFV is generally considered to be vote fraud.  When in
doubt, ask the votetaker.


