>>typewriters out there, but everyone is now using PC's with word
>>processors.  Why not learn on a system that you will have a much
>>greater chance of using after you leave college?

>The equation VMS = typewriter and Unix = word processor is wrong.

Literally, Yes.  It is wrong.  But, I was trying to make an analogy.
Both VMS and Unix will do what you need them to do, just like a
typewriter and a word processor with both teach you how to type.  The
difference between a typewriter and a computer (that I was refering
to), concerned which one was used more in the business world.  If you
want to learn how to type, you might as well learn how on a computer,
since you will most likely be using a computer after school, and not a
typewriter.  I was making this analogy with OS's.  A person will be
much more likely to use Unix in the business world than VMS.  If they
are going to use Unix, then it would be best that they learn Unix in
school.

>People may get familiar with Unix if they run Posix, that comes nearly
>without cost with VMS.

Why run Posix on top of VMS instead of simply using Unix?  You would
miss much of the learning about the Unix Operating System doing so.  If
the business world runs Posix on top of VMS, then the students should
learn in that environment.  But, if the business world uses Unix (more
that Posix/VMS), then the students should learn in Unix.  Too many
students get bachelors degree with no real knowledge of how computers
are used in businesses.

>|>BTW, does anyone have statistics on how much Unix is used verses VMS?
>
>The statistic is meaningless in my opinion. Only the applications you are
>running should direct your decision.

Once you graduate, you will most likely use the applications which are
used the most in the buisness field.  (duh)  That is why the statistics
are important.  It shows what applications you will be running,  And
the applications you will be running should direct your decision.

