Farai Nyoni (farai@nynexst.com) wrote:
> I'm not a regular C programmer, but would like to execute a C shell
> command from within a C program ( setenv VARIABLE XXX). The error I get
> after execution is "setenv: not found"
> when I echo the shell before this command I get "/bin/csh"
> but after I execute the setenv command I get "/bin/sh"

Environment variables are attributes of the process.  They are set by the
command interpreter, and are not external programs.  Thus, you cannot do a
system() on "setenv" because there is no such setenv program; it lives inside
of csh.

To set an environment variable from C:

  int status;
  status = putenv("VARNAME=value");

and to read one:

  char var_p;
  var_p = getenv("VARNAME");


> Is BOURNE the only environment I can operate in or can I change the
> operating shell.  

Nope.  Environment variables are attributes of all processes, but different
shells let you change them differently.

--
Scott J. Tringali, tringali@ultranet.com
