BTW the actual statistics are not nearly as dramatic as the popular
press would have you believe.  At one point, the approximate number of
VMS _users_ was estimated at 10 million, more or less.  This was based on
things like number of terminal servers, etc. in the environment.  In general,
OpenVMS systems have a lot more users than Unix systems do (there are
exceptions).  The number of Unix systems shipped last year was less than
1 million, the overwhelming majority being workstations (more or less
single user).  Much of the X Terminal market and the X server for PC
market is part of the OpenVMS market (particularly with Excursion/Pathworks).
(SCO shipped most of the Unix server licenses; Sun, the most workstations.)

The Unix vs VMS/OpenVMS installed base ratio appears to differ by a factor
less than 10 rather 100.  There are a bunch of PCs running things like
Linux that may slightly alter the gross population counts some, but have
little impact on commercial usage.  (Not a putdown of Linux.  We support
it too)


