We've been down this road as well.

My advice would be to just buy a bigger machine.

The Digital Unix tools, as you've noted with the NIS/C2 problems, do
not seem to scale well in the typical University environment, which
you characterize well as the "lot of users mostly doing rather simple
things."

Having outgrown our machine, we purchased an SGI Challenge, and have
been intending to use it as an NFS/Web/Pop/Imap server, integrated
to our Digital Unix box via NIS and NFS. It has been, to date, a
spectacular failure:

	- NFS's didn't interoperate without a slew of patches
	- NIS on Digital Unix was broken
	- NIS/C2 didn't interoperate on Digital Unix

I hindsight, we'd have been much better off with just a bigger Alpha.
That was not, at the time, politically feasible for us, as Digital
seriously embarrassed itself with an utterly incompetent response
to a catastrophic series of hardware/software problems we had.

If you do opt for a multiple machine route, I advise serious testing
with a realistic load. You'll find problems in no other way.

