A disk on one of our systems (DU 3.2c) had to be replaced on Monday.  With
both the new disk and the old disk installed, I did the standard ...

	newfs
	dump | restore

... sort of thing to copy filesystems from the old drive (still alive, but
clearly on its last legs) to the new one.  I was surprised to see messages
from dump, after it had been running only a few minutes, saying things like

	dump: 16.82% done -- finished in 741:52

(The numbers are made up, but are close to what they actually were.)  So at
some level, at least, there was some confusion.  I do recall that when the
system came up (after being taken down to hook up the new drive) the date
was off by a day.

I was in a rush to get the system back on the air, so I didn't poke around
as I'd have liked, to characterize the problem(s) that one sees without the
patch.  I just installed the patch, and brought the system back up.

However, I do have a question, for whichever Digit(s) might care to answer.
A friend asked ...

      Hmmm.  On reading the patch description again, I am even more
      puzzled.  I can understand changing the date command, but why 
      does the kernel care about leap years?

Indeed, why?  Traditionally, the kernel kept track only of GMT (or UCT, or
whatever it's called these days), relative to the beginning of The Epoch,
and anything else was handled by various library routines.

