From: hedrick@geneva.rutgers.edu (Charles Hedrick)
Subject: Re: What *specific* version of Digital Unix am I running? 3.0 3.0B?
Date: 1996/03/08
Message-ID: <4hored$ka2@geneva.rutgers.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 142157678
references: <4higkm$gls@sundog.tiac.net>
organization: Rutgers University LCSR
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

cgf@bbc.com (Chris Faylor) writes:

>I would like to install the clock patches that have been discussed here
>but I'm embarrassed to admit that I don't remember exactly what version
>of OSF/1 I'm running.  I have one machine running 3.0?  and one running
>3.2?.  'uname -a' does not report a letter after the 3.n on either
>system so am I safe in assuming that I'm running vanilla 3.0 and 3.2 or
>is there some other way to tell that I've forgotten?

In general /etc/motd should show your actual version.  If it doesn't,
the following will work, though there's probably a more elegant approach:

> strings /vmunix | grep UNIX
Digital UNIX V3.2C  (Rev. 148); Thu Feb 29 14:12:48 EST 1996
UNIX_error
>

The first line is what should be in /etc/motd.  This string seems to
be built from /usr/sys/conf/version.*.  So version.variant has C in it
for our situation.  The string itself is in /usr/sys/MACHINE/vers.c.
where MACHINE is the place where your kernel was built, by default the
name of your machine.



