Subject: cc -proto[is] produces fubar results?
Date: 1996/03/10
Message-ID: <4hv1oc$g8i@news.iastate.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 142248107
organization: Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa USA
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.ultrix,comp.unix.osf.osf1

(I get identical results under Ultrix V4.3a and Digital Unix V3.2a)

Doing: "cc -protoi sample.c" (see sample.c below) gives a sample.H of:

extern int (int (* )argc , char * * argv)ifunc(struct xcmd * tbl , char * item);

It seems to me that this isn't even close to legal C, right?

------------------------------- sample.c ---------------------------------
struct xcmd {
        char *  xc_name;
        int (*  xc_ifunc)();
};

/*
 * declare ifunc as function (tbl as pointer to struct xcmd,
 *                            item as pointer to char) returning
 *       pointer to function (argc as int,
 *                            argv as pointer to pointer to char) returning int
 */
int ( * ifunc (
        struct xcmd *   tbl,
        char *          item
) ) (
        int     argc,
        char ** argv
) {
        struct xcmd *   xc;

        for (xc = tbl; xc->xc_name != (char*)0; ++xc) {
                if (strcasecmp(item, xc->xc_name) == 0) break;
        }
        return xc->xc_ifunc;
}
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
