Steve Lionel wrote:
> 
> In article <EDANA.96Mar6122422@allegro.cs.tufts.edu>,
> edana@allegro.cs.tufts.edu (Eric Dana) writes:
> |>Is there a way to get the model name of a system from C?
> |>For example: model=4000/500
> 
> Call the SYS$GETSYI system service, asking for the SYI$_HW_NAME item code.
> --

Gaak! Sounds like VMS ;-)

The Unix equivalent is getsysinfo(2).

Although this call won't give you exactly the model # that appears on
the outside of the box, you can get the CPU type (field GSI_CPU), and
the processor type (field GSI_PROC_TYPE, as defined in
<machine/cpuconf.h>).

Here's a code snipet for a similar operation (to get the amount of
physical memory):

#include <sys/sysinfo.h>
#include <alpha/hal_sysinfo.h>

int physmem;
getsysinfo ( GSI_PHYSMEM, (caddr_t)&physmem, sizeof (physmem));



